### A · What feminism is

1. **Feminism** is the belief in **equal rights and chances** for women and men, together with **action** to achieve it — both a **theory** and a **practice**.
2. Feminist perspectives are distinctive because they **emphasise the centrality of gender** (MCQ 2025).
3. **Kamla Bhasin and Nighat Said Khan** (==1986==) defined feminism as "an awareness of women's oppression and exploitation in society, at work and within the family, and conscious action by **women and men** to change this situation".
4. **bell hooks** (*Feminism Is for Everybody*, ==2000==): feminism is "a movement to end **sexism**, sexist exploitation, and oppression".
5. **Rebecca West** (==1913==) said people called her a feminist whenever she refused to be treated as "a **doormat** or a prostitute".
6. The word *féminisme* began in **France**; **Hubertine Auclert** called herself a feminist in ==1882==.
7. The "**wave**" metaphor was popularised by **Martha Weinman Lear** in ==1968== ("The Second Feminist Wave").
8. The **Seneca Falls Convention** (New York, ==1848==) is taken as the start of **first-wave** feminism.
9. **Rebecca Walker** announced a "**Third Wave**" in ==1992==; the **fourth wave** (from about ==2012==) is **digital**.
10. **Tarabai Shinde** wrote *Stri Purush Tulana* (==1882==), an early South Asian attack on double standards.
11. **Begum Rokeya Sakhawat Hossain** wrote ***Sultana's Dream*** (==1905==), imagining a "Ladyland" run by women.
12. **The Second Sex** (==1949==) was written by **Simone de Beauvoir** (MCQs 2016, 2019, 2025).
13. The **Quaid-e-Azam** said at Aligarh (==1944==): "No nation can rise to the height of glory unless your **women are side by side** with you."

### B · Liberal feminism

14. The theory that men and women should be **treated equally** is **liberal feminism** (MCQ 2023).
15. Liberal feminism rests on **Enlightenment liberalism**: individuals have **reason** and **natural rights**.
16. Its remedy is **reform** within the system — equal laws, education and opportunity — not revolution.
17. **Mary Wollstonecraft** wrote ***A Vindication of the Rights of Woman*** (==1792==), answering **Rousseau's *Émile*** (==1762==).
18. Wollstonecraft argued that women seem weak because they are denied a **rational education**.
19. **Olympe de Gouges** wrote the *Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen* (==1791==).
20. **Elizabeth Cady Stanton** and **Lucretia Mott** drafted the **Declaration of Sentiments** at Seneca Falls (==1848==).
21. **John Stuart Mill** wrote ***The Subjection of Women*** (==1869==), influenced by **Harriet Taylor Mill**.
22. Mill proposed in ==1867== to replace "man" with "**person**" in the Reform Bill to give women the vote.
23. **Betty Friedan** wrote ***The Feminine Mystique*** (==1963==) (MCQs 2024, 2025) — "**the problem that has no name**".
24. Friedan co-founded the **National Organization for Women (NOW)** in ==1966==.
25. **Susan Moller Okin** (*Justice, Gender, and the Family*, ==1989==) argued the **family itself must be just**.
26. **Martha Nussbaum** (==2000==) applied the **capabilities approach** to women's development.
27. The main criticism of liberal feminism: it **ignores structures** and the **private sphere**, and speaks for middle-class women.

### C · Radical feminism

28. "Radical" comes from Latin ***radix***, "**root**": patriarchy is the root of oppression.
29. **Replacing patriarchy** is a **radical** idea (MCQ 2019).
30. **Kate Millett** was a **radical feminist** (MCQs 2016, 2023); she wrote ***Sexual Politics*** (==1970==).
31. Millett defined "**sexual politics**" as **power relations** between the sexes and made "**patriarchy**" the key concept.
32. **The Dialectic of Sex** (==1970==) was written by **Shulamith Firestone** (MCQ 2016).
33. Firestone called women a "**sex class**" and located oppression in **reproduction**.
34. "**The personal is political**" is the title of **Carol Hanisch**'s essay (written ==1969==, published ==1970==).
35. **Consciousness-raising** groups turned "personal" problems into **shared political** awareness.
36. **Redstockings** (==1969==) declared: "Women are an **oppressed class**."
37. **Susan Brownmiller** (*Against Our Will*, ==1975==) described **rape** as a tool that keeps all women in fear.
38. **Catharine MacKinnon** (==1979==) argued that **sexual harassment** is **sex discrimination**.
39. **Adrienne Rich** wrote "**Compulsory Heterosexuality** and Lesbian Existence" (==1980==).
40. Tong divides radical feminists into **radical-libertarian** (Firestone) and **radical-cultural** (Daly, Rich).
41. The first modern **women's shelter** opened in **Chiswick, London**, in ==1971== — a radical-feminist legacy.
42. "***Mera Jism Meri Marzi***" ("My body, my choice") of the **Aurat March** (from ==2018==) echoes radical feminism's demand for **bodily autonomy**.

### D · Marxist and socialist feminism

43. Marxist feminism traces women's oppression to **capitalism and private property**.
44. **Friedrich Engels** wrote ***The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State*** (==1884==).
45. Engels called the rise of private property and father-right "the **world historical defeat of the female sex**".
46. Engels drew on **Lewis Henry Morgan**'s *Ancient Society* (==1877==).
47. **August Bebel** wrote ***Woman and Socialism*** (==1879==).
48. **Clara Zetkin** proposed **International Women's Day** at **Copenhagen** in ==1910==; the UN began marking it on **8 March** in ==1975==.
49. **Alexandra Kollontai** pushed for communal kitchens and childcare in the **Soviet Union**.
50. **Margaret Benston** (==1969==) argued that unpaid **housework is real production**.
51. **Dalla Costa and Selma James** (==1972==) said housework produces the key commodity — **the worker**; this led to **Wages for Housework** (==1972==).
52. **Silvia Federici** (==1975==): "They say it is love. We say it is **unwaged work**."
53. **Social reproduction** means the unpaid daily and generational work that **keeps workers alive and able to work**.
54. The **reserve army of labour** is a pool of workers — often women — **hired in booms and dropped in slumps**.
55. **Socialist feminism** holds that **capitalism and patriarchy together** oppress women.
56. **Juliet Mitchell** (*Woman's Estate*, ==1971==) named **four structures**: production, reproduction, sexuality and socialisation of children.
57. **Heidi Hartmann** wrote "**The Unhappy Marriage of Marxism and Feminism**" (==1979==) — **dual-systems theory**.
58. Hartmann called Marxism "**sex-blind**" and cited the male "**family wage**" as capitalism and patriarchy working together.
59. **Zillah Eisenstein** (==1979==) coined "**capitalist patriarchy**"; **Iris Marion Young** (==1980==) proposed **unified-systems** theory.
60. **Maria Mies** (==1986==) described the "**housewifisation**" of women's work worldwide.
61. **Sylvia Walby** (==1990==) named **six structures** of patriarchy: paid work, household production, culture, sexuality, violence and the state.
62. **Arlie Hochschild** (==1989==) named the "**second shift**" — housework after paid work.
63. Pakistan's **Lady Health Worker Programme** began in ==1994==; **Sindh** passed the first **Home-Based Workers Act** in ==2018==.

### E · Psychoanalytic feminism

64. Psychoanalytic feminism roots women's oppression in the **unconscious** and **early childhood**.
65. **Sigmund Freud** said "**Anatomy is destiny**" (==1924==), adapting a phrase of Napoleon.
66. Freud's **Oedipus complex**: the boy identifies with the father; the girl develops **penis envy** (==1925==).
67. Freud claimed women have a **weaker superego** (conscience).
68. **Karen Horney** ("The Flight from Womanhood", ==1926==) proposed **womb envy** — men envy women's power to give birth.
69. Horney said girls envy men's **social power**, not the penis itself.
70. **Juliet Mitchell** (*Psychoanalysis and Feminism*, ==1974==): Freud **described** patriarchy; he did not **recommend** it.
71. **Dorothy Dinnerstein** (*The Mermaid and the Minotaur*, ==1976==) linked male domination to fear of the **all-powerful mother**.
72. **Nancy Chodorow** (*The Reproduction of Mothering*, ==1978==): girls develop **relational** selves, boys **separate** — so mothering reproduces gender.
73. Chodorow's and Dinnerstein's remedy is **shared (dual) parenting**.
74. **Carol Gilligan** (*In a Different Voice*, ==1982==) contrasted the **ethic of care** with Kohlberg's **ethic of justice**.
75. **Jacques Lacan** placed the child's entry into language under the "**Law of the Father**" — the **symbolic order**.
76. **Luce Irigaray** wrote *Speculum of the Other Woman* (==1974==) and *This Sex Which Is Not One* (==1977==).
77. **Julia Kristeva** contrasted the maternal **semiotic** with the paternal **symbolic** (==1974==).
78. **Hélène Cixous** presented **écriture féminine** in "The Laugh of the Medusa" (==1975==) (MCQ 2024).

### F · Men's feminism

79. **Men's feminism** (pro-feminism) is men's support for gender equality and analysis of how **patriarchy harms men** too.
80. **Frederick Douglass** was the only African-American to sign the Seneca Falls **Declaration of Sentiments** (==1848==).
81. **Condorcet** wrote *On the Admission of Women to the Rights of Citizenship* (==1790==).
82. **Sayyid Mumtaz Ali** of Lahore wrote ***Huquq-un-Niswan*** (==1898==); **Qasim Amin** wrote *The Liberation of Women* (==1899==).
83. **R. W. Connell** (*Masculinities*, ==1995==) named the **patriarchal dividend** — men's gain from women's subordination.
84. **Michael Kimmel** described manhood as a test performed before **other men** (*Manhood in America*, ==1996==).
85. **bell hooks** (*The Will to Change*, ==2004==) argued patriarchy **wounds men emotionally**.
86. The **White Ribbon Campaign** began in **Canada** in ==1991==, after the **Montreal massacre** of ==1989==.
87. **UN Women** launched **HeForShe** on ==20 September 2014== (Emma Watson's speech).
88. The **MenEngage Alliance** (==2004==) is a global network working with **men and boys**.
89. **Robert Bly**'s *Iron John* (==1990==) led the **mythopoetic** men's movement; **men's rights** groups are generally **anti-feminist**.

### G · Postmodern and post-structural feminism

90. **Post-structural feminism** emphasises **the contingent and discursive nature of identities** (MCQs 2018, 2023).
91. **Contingent** = not fixed, could be otherwise; **discursive** = **made by language and discourse**.
92. **Gender equality constructed through literature** is associated with **postmodern feminism** (MCQ 2019).
93. **Lyotard** (*The Postmodern Condition*, ==1979==) defined the postmodern as "**incredulity toward metanarratives**".
94. **Derrida**'s **deconstruction** (==1967==) unsettles ranked binaries such as **man/woman**.
95. **Foucault** joined **power and knowledge**: discourses define what is "normal".
96. Postmodern feminism holds there is **no universal "woman"** — the stance called **anti-essentialism**.
97. **Judith Butler** (*Gender Trouble*, ==1990==): gender is **performative**.
98. **Jane Flax** (==1987==) and **Joan Scott** (==1986==, ==1988==) were early postmodern feminist writers; Scott deconstructed **equality versus difference**.
99. **Donna Haraway** wrote "**A Cyborg Manifesto**" (==1985==).
100. **Seyla Benhabib** (==1995==) warned that the "**death of the subject**" leaves no one to act.
101. **Martha Nussbaum** attacked Butler in "**The Professor of Parody**" (==1999==).
102. **Strategic essentialism** (Spivak) means **temporary strategic unity around an identity** (MCQ 2026).
103. **Kishwar Naheed**'s poem "**We Sinful Women**" became an anthem of Pakistan's 1980s women's movement.

### H · Black feminism, intersectionality and standpoint

104. **Sojourner Truth**'s "**Ain't I a Woman?**" speech was given at **Akron, Ohio**, in ==1851==.
105. The **Combahee River Collective** (==1977==) described **interlocking** oppressions experienced **simultaneously**.
106. **Black feminism** focuses on **race, class and gender** together — "**all of these**" (MCQ 2025).
107. **Kimberlé Crenshaw** coined **intersectionality** in ==1989== ("Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex").
108. Intersectionality originated from discussions on **gender and race** (MCQ 2024).
109. Crenshaw's example was ***DeGraffenreid v. General Motors*** (==1976==), where Black women fell **between** race and sex claims.
110. **Patricia Hill Collins** developed the **matrix of domination** (*Black Feminist Thought*, ==1990==) (MCQ 2026).
111. **Alice Walker** coined **womanism** (==1983==); **Audre Lorde** wrote "The **Master's Tools** Will Never Dismantle the Master's House".
112. **Standpoint theory** argues that **knowledge is socially situated** (MCQ 2026).
113. **Nancy Hartsock** ("The Feminist Standpoint", ==1983==), **Sandra Harding** ("**strong objectivity**") and **Dorothy Smith** are standpoint theorists.

### I · Postcolonial, eco- and Muslim feminism, and other terms

114. **Chandra Talpade Mohanty** ("**Under Western Eyes**", ==1984==) criticised the Western image of a single "**Third World Woman**".
115. **Gayatri Spivak** ("**Can the Subaltern Speak?**", ==1988==): "White men are saving brown women from brown men."
116. **Françoise d'Eaubonne** coined **ecofeminism** in ==1974==.
117. **Vandana Shiva** wrote *Staying Alive* (==1988==); the **Chipko** tree-hugging movement began in ==1973==.
118. **Bina Agarwal** (==1992==) proposed **feminist environmentalism**: women's link to nature comes from their **material work**, not biology.
119. **Amina Wadud** wrote ***Qur'an and Woman*** (==1992==); **Fatima Mernissi** wrote ***The Veil and the Male Elite*** (==1987==).
120. **Asma Barlas** ("*Believing Women" in Islam*, ==2002==) and **Riffat Hassan** are Pakistani-born Muslim feminist scholars.
121. **Musawah**, a global movement for equality in Muslim family law, was launched in ==2009==.
122. The **patriarchal bargain** was elaborated by **Deniz Kandiyoti** ("Bargaining with Patriarchy", ==1988==) (MCQs 2026).
123. **Elisabeth Schüssler Fiorenza** coined **kyriarchy** (==1992==) to describe **interlocking structures of domination** (MCQ 2026).
124. **Elaine Showalter** coined **gynocriticism** (==1979==) — the study of **women writers** (MCQ 2024).

### J · Comparisons and Pakistan

125. **Radical** feminism: **patriarchy alone** is the root; **socialist** feminism: **patriarchy and capitalism** together.
126. **Liberal** feminism **reforms** the system; **radical** and **Marxist** feminism seek to **transform** it.
127. Pakistan's Constitution guarantees non-discrimination on the basis of sex in **Article 25**, and women's full participation in **Article 34**.
128. The best answer to "Which feminism suits Pakistan?" is a **plural** blend: liberal law, Islamic legitimacy, socialist economics, radical vigilance on violence, men as allies, and intersectional targeting.
